  <div class="grid grid-3 cats">
    <div class="card"><span class="tag tag-water">Category 1</span><h3>Clean water</h3><p class="muted small">From a sanitary source: a broken supply line, an overflowing sink with no contaminants, a water heater, melting ice or snow.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="tag tag-sage" style="background:var(--amber-soft);color:var(--amber-ink)">Category 2</span><h3>Gray water</h3><p class="muted small">Significant contamination that could cause illness: washing machine or dishwasher discharge, a toilet overflow with urine but no feces, an aquarium, a failed sump.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><span class="tag tag-signal">Category 3</span><h3>Black water</h3><p class="muted small">Grossly contaminated: sewage, toilet overflow with feces, rising floodwater from rivers or streams, and water that has sat long enough to grow microbes.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Why the category matters</h2>
  <p><strong>Category 1</strong> jobs are mostly about drying. Carpet, padding and drywall can often be saved if extraction and drying start quickly.</p>
  <p><strong>Category 2</strong> adds cleaning and antimicrobial treatment. Carpet padding is usually replaced, and porous materials that soaked it up may need to come out.</p>
  <p><strong>Category 3</strong> is a contamination job. Technicians wear protective equipment, porous materials that the water touched (carpet, pad, drywall, insulation, upholstered furniture) are generally removed and discarded, and everything left is cleaned and sanitized. Children, older adults, pregnant people and anyone with a weakened immune system should stay out until it's done.</p>

  <h2>Water gets dirtier as it sits</h2>
  <p>Category is not fixed. Clean water picks up contaminants from flooring, wall cavities and dust, and microbes multiply in warm, still water. A clean supply-line leak left for a few days can become Category 2 or 3 water. That's the practical reason to start extraction the same day.</p>

  <h2>Classes: how much is wet</h2>
  <p>The S500 standard also sorts jobs into classes based on how much material got wet and how hard it will be to dry. The class drives how much drying equipment goes in and how long it runs.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Class 1:</strong> a small area, with little water absorbed into porous materials.</li>
    <li><strong>Class 2:</strong> a significant share of the room is wet, such as carpet and the lower part of walls.</li>
    <li><strong>Class 3:</strong> most of the room's surfaces are wet, often because water came from above and soaked ceilings, walls, insulation and floors.</li>
    <li><strong>Class 4:</strong> specialty drying. Water is held deep in dense materials like hardwood, plaster, brick or concrete, which take longer and need different equipment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions to ask the restoration company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>What category and class did you assign, and why?</li>
    <li>Which materials can be dried in place, and which need to be removed?</li>
    <li>Will you take and record moisture readings every day until the job is dry?</li>
    <li>Are your technicians IICRC certified in water damage restoration?</li>
  </ul>
